  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/9ad934c88b753dd418e66028bebc341e5d2115dc239ed66b9743d028656d1b96.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $\u0394$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $\u0394?$",
  "worked_solution": "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $\u0394$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\n\u0394={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/5c5f652a4324d6d2ee99d5dcf5d5f3d7be69fd794ebfcd8b6b0a9300d39dca20.png)",
